  "summary": "Building on the previous ideation rounds, I propose a pragmatic incremental approach that avoids the risky 'big refactor first' path. The three sub-pipelines (planning loop, spec writing, impl/test/review loop) are already structurally separated in _run_pipeline_impl as sequential blocks gated by stage checks (lines 1485-1612 in phases.py). Rather than refactoring them into formally independent units with data contracts before adding configuration, we can exploit this existing structure by introducing a board-level 'pipeline_mode' config field with three initial values: 'full' (current behavior), 'plan-only' (stops after plan review passes, moves directly to final-human-approval instead of approved/spec-writing), and 'plan-and-spec' (stops after spec review, skips impl/test/review). The key insight the previous rounds missed is that the coupling problem is actually simpler than described for the 'truncation' case — removing downstream phases from a linear pipeline doesn't create data contract violations because no consumer phase exists to receive empty inputs. The dangerous case is only when you skip a *middle* phase (e.g., skip specs but keep implementing), which these truncation profiles avoid entirely. Implementation is minimal: add a 'pipeline_mode' field to board config (defaulting to 'full'), then add early-return guards in _run_pipeline_impl after each block that check whether the current mode includes the next block. The move-to-approved logic at line 1522 would need a conditional: in 'plan-only' mode, move to final-human-approval instead. This avoids the fragile arbitrary phase-skip approach while still solving the user's stated use case (planning without implementation). Fully composable recipes can come later as a separate enhancement once real usage patterns emerge from these profiles."
